From 348f6e4d01062e4072dcbfb99a41f6580634abc7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sistemas3cecasem <paukarvv@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 12:39:39 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 02/13] lift32: MMX/SSE/SSE2 through one lane-generic emitter
 (simd32.py); unimplemented instructions fail loudly

lift32 had no SSE: movss lifted to a comment and every other xmm
instruction to the UNIMPLEMENTED null statement, so a body skipped it
and carried on with wrong state. Need for Speed Underground 2 has
~3.6k SSE, ~2.5k SSE2 and ~16.7k MMX instructions in reached code.

- tools/lift/simd32.py: MMX (64-bit) and SSE/SSE2 (128-bit) packed
  integer ops share one lane-generic emitter over a V128 union; packed
  and scalar float arithmetic, shuffles, unpacks, packs, shifts, compares
  (comiss/ucomiss as an EFLAGS image), conversions incl. MMX<->SSE,
  movhlps/movlhps, movq2dq/movdq2q, rcp/rsqrt, MXCSR rounding.
  3DNow! lifts to RECOMP_UNIMPL; the host should not report it in cpuid.
- runtime/recomp32/recomp_simd.h: V128 register file and helpers,
  memory through ADDR() and memcpy (no host alignment assumption).
- lift32: anything still unhandled is RECOMP_UNIMPL(va, text), a
  runtime call that names the instruction, instead of a silent ';'.
- difftest: 40 SIMD cases (results stored to memory and compared),
  MXCSR=0x1F80 in Unicorn, SIMD state reset per case. 242/242.
